Setting. U_{0,3} = P^1 \ {0,1,∞} over Q, with S_3 permuting the three punctures. The base point \bar\jmath = -j (j a primitive cube root of 1) is a root of x^2 - x + 1. It is the orbifold point of order 3 of the quotient [U_{0,3}/S_3], i.e. the point j = 0 of the j-line. Notation: M_{0,3} = π_1([U_{0,3,Q}/S_3], -j), the arithmetic, S_3-equivariant fundamental group. π_{0,3}^ = π_1(U_{0,3,\bar Q}) is the free profinite group on 2 generators. \hat M = (π_{0,3}^)_ab ≅ H_1 ⊗ \hat Z ≅ \hat Z^2 (= \hat Z^3/diagonal). Γ_Q acts on \hat M through the cyclotomic character χ and S_3 by permutation. U_∞ = maximal abelian (pro-Fermat) cover of U_{0,3}. Aff = \hat M ⋊ (\hat Z^* × S_3) is the automorphism group of U_∞ over [U_{0,3,Q}/S_3]; the \hat Z^* = Gal(Q^ab/Q) part acts on constants. The canonical map M_{0,3} → Aff of (238) is defined up to \hat M-conjugacy and is fixed by choosing a point of U_∞(\bar Q) above -j.

Top of page. Display (239) recalls an extension 1 → \hat M → G_{p,σ}/μ_G → \hat Z^* × S_3 → 1, built from an earlier group (232). Grothendieck also recalls the canonical homomorphism (218) M_{0,3} → G_{p,σ}/μ_G lifting M_{0,3} → \hat Z^* × S_3. Restricted to the geometric part S^{+^}_{0,3} = π_1([U_{0,3}/S_3]_{\bar Q}, -j), it is the canonical quotient map 'obtained by making π_{0,3}^ abelian'. The target is G_{p,σ}^{S}/μ_G ≅ S^{+^}/μ_S, an extension of S_3 (⊂ \hat Z^* × S_3) by \hat M. (Over C, S^{+^} is presumably the completion of the orbifold group Z/2*Z/3 = PSL_2(Z), but that identification is not on the page.)

Main computation. Grothendieck then puts (238) into the morphism of exact sequences (240):
- top row: 1 → π_{0,3}^ → M_{0,3} → Γ_Q × S_3 → 1;
- bottom row: 1 → \hat M → Aff → \hat Z^* × S_3 → 1;
- right vertical arrow χ × id (from (237));
- left vertical arrow the abelianization (241): π^ → (π^)_ab ≅ ((π_{0,3})_ab)^ ≅ \hat M.

He sets M_1 = M_{0,3}/[π^, π^], i.e. the extension of Γ_Q × S_3 by \hat M obtained by abelianizing the geometric fundamental group. The next page draws the conclusion. M_1 maps isomorphically (five lemma) to the pullback of Aff along χ × id. Aff is a split (semidirect) extension, so M_1 is split. The splittings, up to \hat M-conjugacy, correspond to points of U_∞(\bar Q) above -j. A further quotient M_2 ≅ Aff follows.

Purpose. Grothendieck wants to compare the descent datum of the Fermat tower, i.e. the conjugacy class of liftings M_{0,3} → Aff, with his 'canonical' homomorphisms (217)/(218) into G_{p,σ}/μ_G. The previous page says knowing this class amounts in principle to knowing the Fermat tower up to isomorphism.
